The dot plot shows tree heights.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.7cm,y=0.4cm]
\draw[<->] (4.8,0)--(14.8,0);
\foreach \x in {5,6,...,14} {\draw (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12);\node[below] at (\x,-0.15) {$\x$};}
\foreach \x/\y in {5.1/1,5.3/1,5.4/2,5.6/1,8.2/1,11.0/1,14.5/1} {\fill (\x,\y) circle (2pt);}
\foreach \i/\x/\y/\lx in {1/5.1/1/3.8,2/5.3/1/4.9,3/5.4/2/6.0,4/5.6/1/7.1,5/8.2/1/8.5,6/11.0/1/11.0,7/14.5/1/14.5} {\node[anchor=south,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont] (caption\i) at (\lx,3.5) {$\x$};\draw (\x,{\y+0.2})--(caption\i.south);}
\node[below] at (9.8,-1.1) {Height (m)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Where is the cluster?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(11.4,0);
\foreach \x in {0,0.9,1.8,2.7,3.6,4.5,5.4,6.3,7.2,8.1,9.0,9.9,10.8} {\draw (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12);}
\foreach \x/\lbl in {0.9/200,2.7/300,4.5/400,6.3/500,8.1/600,9.9/700} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.15) {$\lbl$};}
\foreach \x in {0.9,1.8,2.7,3.6,8.1,9.0,9.9} {\node[font=\small] at (\x,0.5) {$\times$};}
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.4,-1.0) {Price in thousands of dollars};
\node[font=\small] at (5.4,1.6) {Unit prices in a block};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Each cross on the number line shows the price of one unit in a block.
Between which two prices is the large gap separating the two groups of prices?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[<->] (-0.6,0)--(11.6,0);
\foreach \x in {0,0.55,1.1,1.65,2.2,2.75,3.3,3.85,4.4,4.95,5.5,6.05,6.6,7.15,7.7,8.25,8.8,9.35,9.9,10.45,11.0} {\draw (\x,-0.1)--(\x,0.1);}
\foreach \x/\lbl in {0/0,1.1/2,2.2/4,3.3/6,4.4/8,5.5/10,6.6/12,7.7/14,8.8/16,9.9/18,11.0/20} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,-0.13) {$\lbl$};}
\foreach \x in {1.65,2.2,2.75,3.3,3.85,9.9} {\node[font=\small] at (\x,0.5) {$\times$};}
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (5.5,-1.0) {Height (m)};
\node[font=\small] at (5.5,1.6) {Heights of six trees};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Each cross on the number line shows the height of one tree in a park.
Which statement correctly identifies the cluster and the large gap?
